### What is the difference between Vector and ArrayList?

Vector and ArrayList are both backed by dynamic arrays, implement the `List` interface, and provide the same operations. The differences are in synchronization, growth strategy, and API age.

| Feature | Vector | ArrayList |
|---|---|---|
| Introduced | Java 1.0 | Java 1.2 |
| Synchronized | Yes (every method) | No |
| Growth | 2x (doubles) | 1.5x |
| Performance | Slow (lock overhead) | Fast |
| Null allowed | Yes | Yes |
| Legacy methods | addElement, elementAt, elements() | Standard Collection API |
| Thread safety | Built-in (coarse-grained) | Not thread-safe |
| Recommendation | Do not use | Use this |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class VectorVsArrayListDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Vector – synchronized, legacy
        Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>();
        vector.addElement("SAV2001"); // legacy method
        String first = vector.elementAt(0); // legacy method
        Enumeration<String> enumeration = vector.elements(); // legacy iterator

        // ArrayList – modern, faster
        List<String> arrayList = new ArrayList<>();
        arrayList.add("SAV2001");
        String firstAL = arrayList.get(0);
        Iterator<String> iterator = arrayList.iterator();

        System.out.println("Vector: " + vector);
        System.out.println("ArrayList: " + arrayList);
    }
}
```

```text
Vector: [SAV2001]
ArrayList: [SAV2001]
```

### Why is Vector slow?

Every public method in Vector is declared `synchronized`. This means that before executing any operation – even `size()` or `get()` – the calling thread must acquire the object's intrinsic lock (monitor).

In single-threaded code, this lock acquisition and release is pure waste. It adds 15-30% overhead compared to ArrayList for the same operations.

In multi-threaded code, the synchronization is too coarse. All threads compete for a single lock on the entire Vector. While one thread is reading, no other thread can read or write. This creates a bottleneck that modern concurrent collections avoid with fine-grained locking.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class SynchronizationOverheadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int iterations = 10_000_000;

        // Vector – synchronized on every operation
        Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>();
        vector.add("SAV2001");
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            vector.get(0); // lock, get, unlock – every time
        }
        long vectorTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        // ArrayList – no synchronization
        ArrayList<String> arrayList = new ArrayList<>();
        arrayList.add("SAV2001");
        start = System.nanoTime();
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            arrayList.get(0); // direct array access – no locking
        }
        long arrayListTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.printf("Vector: %d ms%n", vectorTime / 1_000_000);
        System.out.printf("ArrayList: %d ms%n", arrayListTime / 1_000_000);
        System.out.printf("Vector is ~%.1fx slower%n", (double) vectorTime / arrayListTime);
    }
}
```

```text
Vector: 85 ms
ArrayList: 32 ms
Vector is ~2.7x slower
```

### How is the growth strategy different?

Vector doubles its capacity by default (2x growth). You can also specify a custom increment: `new Vector<>(10, 5)` starts at capacity 10 and grows by 5 each time.

ArrayList grows by approximately 1.5x (the formula `old + old >> 1`).

The 2x growth wastes more memory. After reaching capacity 1024, Vector jumps to 2048 (1024 wasted slots). ArrayList would go to 1536 (512 wasted slots).

| Starting from 10 | Vector growth | ArrayList growth |
|---|---|---|
| Resize 1 | 10 to 20 | 10 to 15 |
| Resize 2 | 20 to 40 | 15 to 22 |
| Resize 3 | 40 to 80 | 22 to 33 |
| Resize 4 | 80 to 160 | 33 to 49 |
| Resize 5 | 160 to 320 | 49 to 73 |
| To reach 1M | ~17 resizes | ~40 resizes |
| Memory at 1M | Up to 2M capacity | Up to 1.5M capacity |

### What is wrong with Stack?

`Stack` extends `Vector`, which means it inherits all of Vector's problems: synchronized every method, 2x growth, legacy API. Additionally, because Stack extends Vector, it exposes all of Vector's methods – including `add(index, elem)`, `remove(index)`, and `get(index)` – which violate the LIFO contract of a stack.

Use `ArrayDeque` as a stack instead. It is faster, uses less memory, and does not expose non-stack methods.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class StackVsArrayDequeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BAD – Stack extends Vector (synchronized, legacy)
        Stack<String> legacyStack = new Stack<>();
        legacyStack.push("Step 1: Open account");
        legacyStack.push("Step 2: Deposit 50000");
        // Stack exposes Vector methods – breaks stack contract!
        legacyStack.add(0, "INSERTED AT BOTTOM"); // not stack behavior!

        // GOOD – ArrayDeque as stack
        Deque<String> modernStack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        modernStack.push("Step 1: Open account");
        modernStack.push("Step 2: Deposit 50000");
        System.out.println("Pop: " + modernStack.pop());
        System.out.println("Peek: " + modernStack.peek());
        // No add(index) method – clean stack API
    }
}
```

```text
Pop: Step 2: Deposit 50000
Peek: Step 1: Open account
```

### What are the modern alternatives for thread safety?

| Need | Solution |
|---|---|
| Single-threaded | ArrayList (no synchronization) |
| Simple thread safety | Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>()) |
| Read-heavy concurrent | CopyOnWriteArrayList |
| Queue (producer-consumer) | ConcurrentLinkedQueue or LinkedBlockingQueue |
| Stack (concurrent) | ConcurrentLinkedDeque |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ThreadSafeAlternativesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Option 1: synchronizedList – simple but slow under contention
        List<String> syncList = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>());
        syncList.add("SAV2001");

        // Option 2: CopyOnWriteArrayList – ideal for read-heavy scenarios
        // Writes copy the entire array (expensive)
        // Reads are lock-free (fast)
        List<String> cowList = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
        cowList.add("SAV2001");
        // 10,000 readers + 1 writer → perfect for event listener lists

        // Iteration is always safe (uses snapshot)
        for (String acc : cowList) {
            System.out.println("Safe iteration: " + acc);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Safe iteration: SAV2001
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Using Vector "because it is thread-safe"

```java
// WRONG – Vector's synchronization is too coarse and slow
List<String> accounts = new Vector<>();

// Also WRONG – even Vector is not safe for compound operations
Vector<String> v = new Vector<>();
if (!v.contains("SAV2001")) {
    v.add("SAV2001"); // RACE CONDITION! Another thread may add between check and add
}
// Vector synchronizes individual methods, not compound operations.
// You still need external synchronization for check-then-act patterns.

// CORRECT for compound operations
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Boolean> accounts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
accounts.putIfAbsent("SAV2001", Boolean.TRUE); // atomic check-and-add
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Vector's compound action problem.** Synchronizing individual methods does not make compound operations thread-safe. "Check if contains, then add" is still a race condition with Vector because the lock is released between `contains()` and `add()`. You need external synchronization or atomic operations.

- **JIT optimization of locks.** On HotSpot, the JIT compiler can sometimes eliminate locks on thread-local objects (lock elision) or replace contended locks with faster alternatives (lock coarsening). This reduces Vector's overhead in some cases, but it is not reliable enough to depend on.

- **History.** Vector was in Java from version 1.0 (1996). The Collections Framework was added in Java 1.2 (1998). Vector was retrofitted to implement `List`, which is why it has both legacy methods (elementAt) and modern methods (get). The same happened with Hashtable implementing `Map`.

- **Stack is a design mistake.** Stack should have been a interface or a composed class, not a subclass of Vector. By extending Vector, Stack inherits all Vector methods including random access – violating the LIFO abstraction. This is a textbook example of inheritance misuse.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Can you make ArrayList thread-safe?" – Yes, wrap with Collections.synchronizedList() or use CopyOnWriteArrayList.
- "What is the growth factor of Vector?" – 2x by default, configurable via constructor.
- "Why is Stack a bad design?" – It extends Vector, exposing non-stack operations and inheriting synchronization overhead.
- "What is CopyOnWriteArrayList?" – A thread-safe list where writes copy the entire array, making reads lock-free.
